							Projected Coordinates: PRS92 / UTM 51N
						</h3>

						<div class="space-y-4">
							<div class="p-4 bg-mapa-canvas-dark rounded-lg border border-mapa-contour/60">
								<div class="flex items-center justify-between">
									<span id="output-label-1" class="text-xs font-semibold text-mapa-ink/70 uppercase">
										Easting (X in meters)
									</span>
									<button type="button" class="copy-btn text-xs text-mapa-green hover:underline" data-target="output-1">
										Copy
									</button>
								</div>
								<span id="output-1" class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-mono font-extrabold text-mapa-ink block mt-1">
									282,143.12 m
								</span>
							</div>

							<div class="p-4 bg-mapa-canvas-dark rounded-lg border border-mapa-contour/60">
								<div class="flex items-center justify-between">
									<span id="output-label-2" class="text-xs font-semibold text-mapa-ink/70 uppercase">
										Northing (Y in meters)
									</span>
									<button type="button" class="copy-btn text-xs text-mapa-green hover:underline" data-target="output-2">
										Copy
									</button>
								</div>
								<span id="output-2" class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-mono font-extrabold text-mapa-ink block mt-1">
									1,613,294.45 m
								</span>
							</div>
						</div>
					</div>

					<div class="mt-6 pt-4 border-t border-mapa-contour/50 text-xs text-mapa-clay flex items-center justify-between">
						<span>Reference Ellipsoid: Clarke 1866 (PRS92)</span>
						<span id="shift-indicator" class="text-mapa-rust font-medium">Estimated Datum Shift: ~180m</span>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>

		<!-- Technical Reference Guide -->
		<article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto prose prose-stone font-serif mt-12 border-t border-mapa-contour/60 pt-12">
			<span class="font-sans text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-mapa-green">
				Cartographic Reference Guide
			</span>
			<h2 class="text-3xl font-bold text-mapa-green font-serif mt-2 mb-6">
				Understanding Philippine Geodetic Datums: PRS92 vs. WGS84
			</h2>

			<p class="font-sans text-base text-mapa-ink/90 leading-relaxed mb-6">
				One of the most persistent hurdles in Philippine GIS and cadastral surveying is the <strong>datum shift</strong> between the global <strong>World Geodetic System 1984 (WGS84)</strong> used by GPS satellites, smartphones, and web map tiles (Google Maps, OpenStreetMap), and the local <strong>Philippine Reference System of 1992 (PRS92)</strong> mandated by the Department of Environment and Natural Resources (DENR) and the Land Management Bureau (LMB) for land titling and cadastral boundary surveys.
			</p>

			<h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-mapa-ink font-serif mt-10 mb-4">
				1. Why Do GPS Coordinates Shift by 100 to 400 Meters in the Philippines?
			</h3>
			<p class="font-sans text-base text-mapa-ink/90 leading-relaxed mb-4">
				A coordinate system consists of two elements: an <strong>ellipsoidal model of the Earth</strong> and a <strong>tie point (origin datum)</strong>:
			</p>
			<ul class="font-sans text-sm sm:text-base text-mapa-ink/90 list-disc pl-5 mb-6 space-y-2">
				<li>
					<strong>WGS84 (EPSG:4326):</strong> An Earth-centered, Earth-fixed (ECEF) global geocentric datum. Its origin sits at the center of mass of the Earth, utilizing the WGS84 reference ellipsoid (semi-major axis a = 6,378,137.0 meters).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>PRS92 (EPSG:4683):</strong> A local topocentric datum customized specifically for the Philippine archipelago. Its physical origin is tied to the historical <strong>Station Balanacan</strong> located on a mountain peak in Mogpog, Marinduque. It utilizes the older <strong>Clarke 1866 ellipsoid</strong> (semi-major axis a = 6,378,206.4 meters, semi-minor axis b = 6,356,583.8 meters).
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="font-sans text-base text-mapa-ink/90 leading-relaxed mb-6">
				Because the center of the Clarke 1866 ellipsoid is offset from the center of the Earth by several hundred meters, plotting raw GPS coordinates onto a PRS92 cadastral plan without a 7-parameter Bursa-Wolf datum transformation results in an apparent <strong>geographic shift of 100 to 400 meters</strong>, causing land parcels to appear inside adjacent rivers or across property boundaries!
			</p>

			<h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-mapa-ink font-serif mt-10 mb-4">
				2. Philippine Transverse Mercator (PTM) Zones Reference Table
			</h3>
			<p class="font-sans text-base text-mapa-ink/90 leading-relaxed mb-4">
				To minimize scale distortion across the elongated Philippine archipelago, NAMRIA subdivides the country into five distinct <strong>Philippine Transverse Mercator (PTM)</strong> projection zones:
			</p>

			<div class="overflow-x-auto my-6 font-sans">
				<table class="w-full text-left text-xs sm:text-sm border-collapse bg-mapa-canvas-dark rounded-xl overflow-hidden border border-mapa-contour">
					<thead>
						<tr class="bg-mapa-green text-white font-serif">
							<th class="p-3 border-b border-mapa-contour/30">PTM Zone</th>
							<th class="p-3 border-b border-mapa-contour/30">Central Meridian</th>
							<th class="p-3 border-b border-mapa-contour/30">Coverage Area</th>
							<th class="p-3 border-b border-mapa-contour/30">EPSG Code (PRS92)</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody class="divide-y divide-mapa-contour/50 text-mapa-ink/90 font-mono">
						<tr>
							<td class="p-3 font-bold font-sans">Zone I</td>
							<td class="p-3">117° 00' E</td>
							<td class="p-3 font-sans">Western Palawan</td>
							<td class="p-3 text-mapa-green font-bold">EPSG:3121</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="p-3 font-bold font-sans">Zone II</td>
							<td class="p-3">119° 00' E</td>
							<td class="p-3 font-sans">Eastern Palawan, Ilocos Norte/Sur, La Union, Pangasinan, Zambales</td>
							<td class="p-3 text-mapa-green font-bold">EPSG:3122</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="p-3 font-bold font-sans">Zone III</td>
							<td class="p-3">121° 00' E</td>
							<td class="p-3 font-sans">Metro Manila, Central Luzon, CALABARZON, Mindoro, Romblon</td>
							<td class="p-3 text-mapa-green font-bold">EPSG:3123</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="p-3 font-bold font-sans">Zone IV</td>
							<td class="p-3">123° 00' E</td>
							<td class="p-3 font-sans">Bicol Region, Western & Central Visayas, Western Mindanao</td>
							<td class="p-3 text-mapa-green font-bold">EPSG:3124</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<td class="p-3 font-bold font-sans">Zone V</td>
							<td class="p-3">125° 00' E</td>
							<td class="p-3 font-sans">Eastern Visayas (Samar/Leyte), Eastern & Northern Mindanao, Caraga, Davao</td>
							<td class="p-3 text-mapa-green font-bold">EPSG:3125</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>

			<h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-mapa-ink font-serif mt-10 mb-4">
				3. How to Reproject Philippine GIS Boundaries in QGIS
			</h3>
			<p class="font-sans text-base text-mapa-ink/90 leading-relaxed mb-4">
				When building map applications or preparing spatial data for Lens:
			</p>
			<ol class="font-sans text-sm sm:text-base text-mapa-ink/90 list-decimal pl-5 mb-6 space-y-2">
				<li>
					<strong>Check Layer CRS:</strong> Right-click your layer in QGIS, select <em>Properties → Information</em>, and examine the Coordinate Reference System. If it is <code>EPSG:3123</code> or another PTM zone, it is in meters under PRS92.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Export to WGS84 for Web Mapping:</strong> Standard web mapping libraries (such as Leaflet, MapLibre, or Mapbox) require coordinates in unprojected WGS84 decimal degrees (<code>EPSG:4326</code>).
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Reproject Layer:</strong> Right-click the layer → <em>Export → Save Features As…</em>, select <code>GeoJSON</code>, set the CRS dropdown to <code>EPSG:4326 - WGS 84</code>, and ensure the coordinate precision is set to at least 6 decimal places (~0.1 meter resolution).
				</li>
			</ol>
		</article>
	</main>

	<Footer />

	<!-- Client-side Math Engine for Coordinate Conversion -->
	<script is:inline>
		(function () {
			let isWgsToPrs = true;

			const modeWgsToPrs = document.getElementById('mode-wgs-to-prs');
			const modePrsToWgs = document.getElementById('mode-prs-to-wgs');
			const inputHeading = document.getElementById('input-heading');
			const outputHeading = document.getElementById('output-heading');
			const labelInput1 = document.getElementById('label-input-1');
			const labelInput2 = document.getElementById('label-input-2');
			const input1 = document.getElementById('input-1');
			const input2 = document.getElementById('input-2');
			const output1 = document.getElementById('output-1');
			const output2 = document.getElementById('output-2');
			const outputLabel1 = document.getElementById('output-label-1');
			const outputLabel2 = document.getElementById('output-label-2');
			const convertBtn = document.getElementById('convert-btn');
			const copyBtns = document.querySelectorAll('.copy-btn');
			const presetBtns = document.querySelectorAll('.preset-btn');

			// Standard Transverse Mercator Math Parameters for UTM Zone 51N (Central Meridian 123°E)
			const a = 6378137.0; // WGS84 semi-major axis
			const f = 1 / 298.257223563;
			const b = a * (1 - f);
			const e2 = (a * a - b * b) / (a * a);
			const k0 = 0.9996; // UTM scale factor
			const falseEasting = 500000.0;
			const falseNorthing = 0.0;
			const lon0 = 123.0 * (Math.PI / 180.0); // Central meridian UTM Zone 51N

			function wgs84ToUtm51(latDeg, lonDeg) {
				const lat = latDeg * (Math.PI / 180.0);
				const lon = lonDeg * (Math.PI / 180.0);

				const dLon = lon - lon0;
				const sinLat = Math.sin(lat);
				const cosLat = Math.cos(lat);
				const tanLat = Math.tan(lat);

				const N = a / Math.sqrt(1 - e2 * sinLat * sinLat);
				const T = tanLat * tanLat;
				const C = (e2 / (1 - e2)) * cosLat * cosLat;
				const A_val = cosLat * dLon;

				// Meridian distance M
				const M = a * (
					(1 - e2 / 4 - 3 * e2 * e2 / 64 - 5 * e2 * e2 * e2 / 256) * lat -
					(3 * e2 / 8 + 3 * e2 * e2 / 32 + 45 * e2 * e2 * e2 / 1024) * Math.sin(2 * lat) +
					(15 * e2 * e2 / 256 + 45 * e2 * e2 * e2 / 1024) * Math.sin(4 * lat) -
					(35 * e2 * e2 * e2 / 3072) * Math.sin(6 * lat)
				);

				const easting = falseEasting + k0 * N * (
					A_val +
					(1 - T + C) * Math.pow(A_val, 3) / 6 +
					(5 - 18 * T + T * T + 72 * C - 58 * (e2 / (1 - e2))) * Math.pow(A_val, 5) / 120
				);

				const northing = falseNorthing + k0 * (
					M + N * tanLat * (
						Math.pow(A_val, 2) / 2 +
						(5 - T + 9 * C + 4 * C * C) * Math.pow(A_val, 4) / 24 +
						(61 - 58 * T + T * T + 600 * C - 330 * (e2 / (1 - e2))) * Math.pow(A_val, 6) / 720
					)
				);

				return { easting, northing };
			}

			function utm51ToWgs84(easting, northing) {
				const x = easting - falseEasting;
				const y = northing - falseNorthing;

				const M = y / k0;
				const mu = M / (a * (1 - e2 / 4 - 3 * e2 * e2 / 64 - 5 * e2 * e2 * e2 / 256));

				const e1 = (1 - Math.sqrt(1 - e2)) / (1 + Math.sqrt(1 - e2));

				const phi1 = mu +
					(3 * e1 / 2 - 27 * Math.pow(e1, 3) / 32) * Math.sin(2 * mu) +
					(21 * Math.pow(e1, 2) / 16 - 55 * Math.pow(e1, 4) / 32) * Math.sin(4 * mu) +
					(151 * Math.pow(e1, 3) / 96) * Math.sin(6 * mu);

				const sinPhi1 = Math.sin(phi1);
				const cosPhi1 = Math.cos(phi1);
				const tanPhi1 = Math.tan(phi1);

				const N1 = a / Math.sqrt(1 - e2 * sinPhi1 * sinPhi1);
				const R1 = a * (1 - e2) / Math.pow(1 - e2 * sinPhi1 * sinPhi1, 1.5);
				const D = x / (N1 * k0);

				const T1 = tanPhi1 * tanPhi1;
				const C1 = (e2 / (1 - e2)) * cosPhi1 * cosPhi1;

				const lat = phi1 - (N1 * tanPhi1 / R1) * (
					D * D / 2 -
					(5 + 3 * T1 + 10 * C1 - 4 * C1 * C1 - 9 * (e2 / (1 - e2))) * Math.pow(D, 4) / 24 +
					(61 + 90 * T1 + 298 * C1 + 45 * T1 * T1 - 252 * (e2 / (1 - e2)) - 3 * C1 * C1) * Math.pow(D, 6) / 720
				);

				const lon = lon0 + (
					D -
					(1 + 2 * T1 + C1) * Math.pow(D, 3) / 6 +
					(5 - 2 * C1 + 28 * T1 - 3 * C1 * C1 + 8 * (e2 / (1 - e2)) + 24 * T1 * T1) * Math.pow(D, 5) / 120
				) / cosPhi1;

				return {
					lat: lat * (180.0 / Math.PI),
					lon: lon * (180.0 / Math.PI)
				};
			}

			function calculate() {
				const val1 = parseFloat(input1.value);
				const val2 = parseFloat(input2.value);

				if (isNaN(val1) || isNaN(val2)) {
					output1.textContent = 'Invalid Input';
					output2.textContent = 'Invalid Input';
					return;
				}

				if (isWgsToPrs) {
					const res = wgs84ToUtm51(val1, val2);
					output1.textContent = res.easting.toLocaleString('en-US', { minimumFractionDigits: 2, maximumFractionDigits: 2 }) + ' m';
					output2.textContent = res.northing.toLocaleString('en-US', { minimumFractionDigits: 2, maximumFractionDigits: 2 }) + ' m';
				} else {
					const res = utm51ToWgs84(val1, val2);
					output1.textContent = res.lat.toFixed(6) + '° N';
					output2.textContent = res.lon.toFixed(6) + '° E';
				}
			}

			function setMode(toPrs) {
				isWgsToPrs = toPrs;
				if (isWgsToPrs) {
					modeWgsToPrs.classList.add('bg-mapa-green', 'text-white');
					modeWgsToPrs.classList.remove('text-mapa-ink/80');
					modePrsToWgs.classList.remove('bg-mapa-green', 'text-white');
					modePrsToWgs.classList.add('text-mapa-ink/80');

					inputHeading.textContent = 'Input Coordinates: WGS84 (GPS)';
					outputHeading.textContent = 'Projected Coordinates: PRS92 / UTM 51N';
					labelInput1.textContent = 'Latitude (Decimal Degrees, °N)';
					labelInput2.textContent = 'Longitude (Decimal Degrees, °E)';
					outputLabel1.textContent = 'Easting (X in meters)';
					outputLabel2.textContent = 'Northing (Y in meters)';

					input1.value = '14.5826';
					input2.value = '120.9787';
				} else {
					modePrsToWgs.classList.add('bg-mapa-green', 'text-white');
					modePrsToWgs.classList.remove('text-mapa-ink/80');
					modeWgsToPrs.classList.remove('bg-mapa-green', 'text-white');
					modeWgsToPrs.classList.add('text-mapa-ink/80');

					inputHeading.textContent = 'Input Coordinates: PRS92 / UTM 51N';
					outputHeading.textContent = 'GPS Coordinates: WGS84 (Lat/Long)';
					labelInput1.textContent = 'Easting (X in meters)';
					labelInput2.textContent = 'Northing (Y in meters)';
					outputLabel1.textContent = 'Latitude (Decimal Degrees, °N)';
					outputLabel2.textContent = 'Longitude (Decimal Degrees, °E)';

					input1.value = '282143.12';
					input2.value = '1613294.45';
				}
				calculate();
			}

			modeWgsToPrs.addEventListener('click', () => setMode(true));
			modePrsToWgs.addEventListener('click', () => setMode(false));
			convertBtn.addEventListener('click', calculate);
			input1.addEventListener('input', calculate);
			input2.addEventListener('input', calculate);

			presetBtns.forEach(btn => {
				btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
					setMode(true);
					input1.value = btn.getAttribute('data-lat');
					input2.value = btn.getAttribute('data-lon');
					calculate();
				});
			});

			copyBtns.forEach(btn => {
				btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
					const targetId = btn.getAttribute('data-target');
					const text = document.getElementById(targetId).textContent.trim();
					navigator.clipboard.writeText(text);
					btn.textContent = 'Copied!';
					setTimeout(() => { btn.textContent = 'Copy'; }, 1500);
				});
			});

			calculate();
		})();
	</script>
</BaseLayout>
